puttymod for Debian
====================

This package ships only the GTK GUI client (built from the upstream
"putty" CMake target, renamed to /usr/bin/puttymod). It intentionally
does NOT ship plink/pscp/psftp/puttygen/pageant/psusan: those are
unmodified by this fork and already provided by the official
putty-tools package, so puttymod happily installs alongside a normal
putty/putty-tools install with no file overlap.

Distro-icon licensing -- RESOLVED 2026-08-01
---------------------------------------------
unix/distro_icons.h embeds ~38 small Linux/Unix distribution/vendor
logo icons, sourced during early development, used to decorate the
saved-sessions list based on the remote host's detected OS. All 38
are now traced to a free/open source or are this fork's own original
artwork:
  - 31 to "dashboard-icons" (github.com/homarr-labs/dashboard-icons,
    Apache-2.0) -- confirmed from browser history (fetched via
    jsdelivr CDN from that repo) and by visually diffing the
    compiled-in pixel data against freshly downloaded originals.
  - 3 (elementary, popos, slackware) to Simple Icons
    (simpleicons.org, CC0) -- confirmed by rendering their canonical
    SVGs and matching the glyph shape.
  - 1 (freebsd) to selfhst/icons (github.com/selfhst/icons,
    CC-BY-4.0) -- confirmed by matching the "Beastie" silhouette
    (this fork's copy is a darker recolour of the same shape).
  - 1 (raspbian) to papirus-icon-theme
    (github.com/PapirusDevelopmentTeam/papirus-icon-theme,
    GPL-3.0-or-later, already an official Debian package) --
    confirmed by matching the raspberry silhouette.
  - 1 (astra) REPLACED with original artwork -- Astra Linux's own
    official press kit (astra.ru) has a logo download but states no
    reuse licence at all (a normal commercial trademark, not a
    community-project logo with reuse permission like the others
    above), so it was a real unresolved trademark risk rather than
    just a missing attribution. Swapped for a plain blue "A" on a
    white badge, matching this fork's own flat-badge style (see the
    "question" icon and FEATURES.md's Credits section).
  - 1 (dropbear) is, and always was, this fork's own original artwork
    (a generic "DR" letter-badge) -- never sourced from a third party.
See debian/copyright for the full per-icon list and detail.

Splitting this single generated header back into per-icon source
files, so each icon can carry its own copyright/licence stanza
instead of one catch-all, is still outstanding but no longer blocks
DFSG-freeness.

Local patches
-------------
See /usr/share/doc/puttymod/copyright and the upstream source tree
(link in debian/control's Homepage) for what's changed relative to
stock PuTTY: i18n, per-session status dots, theme presets, encrypted
session export/import, encrypted auto-login password, first-run
welcome dialog with a Tokyo Night default theme.

